00:01:18.831 --> 00:01:19.841
- [Voiceover] Dan asks,

00:01:19.841 --> 00:01:21.617
"How do you prepare
employees transitioning into

00:01:21.617 --> 00:01:24.687
"a leadership role when they've
never managed people before?"

00:01:26.318 --> 00:01:29.418
- Dan, I think one of the big
mistakes a lot of people make

00:01:31.098 --> 00:01:33.330
running organizations as
me, CEO of this company,

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:36.175
is they, eventually
somebody gets into a place

00:01:36.175 --> 00:01:38.555
where they're good enough,
they go into managerial roles,

00:01:38.555 --> 00:01:40.389
so they go from being a
mason to an architect,

00:01:40.389 --> 00:01:42.675
they no longer do the day-in-day-outs,

00:01:42.675 --> 00:01:45.137
they have to architect
people doing it at scale

00:01:45.137 --> 00:01:48.817
and the CEO, or the
business owner, or the boss,

00:01:48.817 --> 00:01:51.847
never put that person in
position along the way

00:01:52.897 --> 00:01:53.791
to do that.

00:01:53.791 --> 00:01:55.922
Like, I'm really curious
how many people at Vayner

00:01:55.922 --> 00:01:58.092
are gonna watch this or
listen to this answer,

00:01:58.092 --> 00:02:01.354
but it may make them start rewinding

00:02:01.354 --> 00:02:03.990
a lot of their day-to-day
things that they may not realize

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.685
that I'm puppeting from here,

00:02:05.685 --> 00:02:07.926
because they're getting
told by their bosses,

00:02:07.926 --> 00:02:11.328
but a lot of people,
whether you're a, you know,

00:02:11.328 --> 00:02:13.034
an account manager or
an account supervisor

00:02:13.034 --> 00:02:16.179
about to become an account
director or group director

00:02:16.179 --> 00:02:17.271
where you're managing more people,

00:02:17.271 --> 00:02:18.478
there's a lot of things I'm doing,

00:02:18.478 --> 00:02:20.358
changing the accounts you work on,

00:02:20.358 --> 00:02:22.517
changing the team that's underneath you,

00:02:22.517 --> 00:02:24.619
there's a lot of puppeteering going on

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:28.799
to really a lot of different, like,

00:02:28.799 --> 00:02:30.586
groups I want them to be a part of,

00:02:31.486 --> 00:02:33.663
just different little
things that are happening

00:02:33.663 --> 00:02:36.146
to create a proxy and a context point

00:02:36.146 --> 00:02:38.643
and a data point on who they would be

00:02:38.643 --> 00:02:39.781
if they were manager.

00:02:39.781 --> 00:02:41.127
And so I think the answer
to this question is,

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:43.983
I prep by prepping them along the way,

00:02:43.983 --> 00:02:45.098
versus ripping off the band-aid,

00:02:45.098 --> 00:02:48.383
and then, I hope they can be a manager.

00:02:48.383 --> 00:02:49.485
You know?

00:02:49.485 --> 00:02:51.158
I think you've gotta prep people,

00:02:51.158 --> 00:02:54.325
and I think you've gotta
give them some context points

00:02:54.325 --> 00:02:55.917
and put them in positions to succeed

00:02:55.917 --> 00:02:58.216
and really, like, know them, too.

00:02:58.216 --> 00:02:59.806
Know if they were babysitters or the head

00:02:59.806 --> 00:03:03.660
of their sorority, or,
you know, how they roll.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.785
Like, really auditing
them as human beings,

00:03:05.785 --> 00:03:08.745
360 outside of the context of
just them being an employee.

00:03:08.745 --> 00:03:10.986
There are all very
important parts, and if you

00:03:10.986 --> 00:03:13.051
can replicate some of the
versions that matter to you,

00:03:13.051 --> 00:03:14.131
a lot of my people, I think,

00:03:14.131 --> 00:03:16.848
are really capable of being
managers from a skill level,

00:03:16.848 --> 00:03:19.042
but they don't have the HREQ,

00:03:19.042 --> 00:03:21.990
they're too type A, they
need to take a step back

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.382
and zen a little bit to let
their employees breathe.

00:03:24.382 --> 00:03:26.762
They need to realize they
can't impose their skills.

00:03:26.762 --> 00:03:28.167
Biggest mistake a lot of managers make

00:03:28.167 --> 00:03:30.280
when they first become
managers, is they try to turn

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.625
everybody underneath them into them,

00:03:32.625 --> 00:03:35.312
versus turning them into the
best version of themselves.

